# 3. DESIGN THE PLATFORM AROUND USER INTENT

The current platform contains many powerful capabilities. The problem to solve is that users should not need to understand the platform's internal structure before they can use it.

Instead, organize the experience around what the user wants to accomplish.

Build a simple primary navigation structure with clear destinations.

A possible direction is:

### A. Home
The starting point, with a clear explanation of what to do first.

### B. Learn
Everything related to discovering and understanding human anatomy and biomedical subjects.

This must make the following accessible:

- 3D Anatomy Explorer
- All learning subjects
- Body systems
- Individual topics and chapters
- Connected Study Workspace
- Syllabus Navigator
- Reference library
- Search and discovery

### C. Practice
Everything related to testing and tracking learning:

- Quizzes
- Practical Lab
- Flashcards
- Revision
- Study Dashboard
- Weak areas
- Bookmarks
- Adaptive study recommendations

### D. Teach
Everything a teacher or professor needs:

- Teacher & Professor Workspace
- Course planning
- Curriculum mapping
- Teaching sequences
- Lecture outlines
- Chapter decks
- Student worksheets
- Classroom tools
- Guided presentations

### E. All Tools
A complete, organized directory containing every existing advanced feature and destination.

Include:

- Relationship Explorer
- Physiology Simulations
- Histology Library
- Clinical Correlations
- Presentation Studio
- Atlas & Pack Manager
- All remaining specialist functionality.

This is a suggested information architecture, not permission to delete or replace existing destinations.

You may refine the exact names and grouping after inspecting the code.

### Navigation requirements

- Keep global search easy to find from anywhere.
- Use clear icons alongside understandable labels.
- Give each category a one-sentence explanation.
- Show the active destination clearly.
- Make dropdowns and mobile navigation predictable.
- Avoid unnecessarily deep navigation.
- Users should generally be able to reach an important feature within no more than three deliberate navigation actions.
- Do not hide advanced features permanently.
- Keep every existing route and supported deep link working.
- Keep mobile navigation organized rather than simply displaying one excessively long list.

A user should understand the difference between Learn, Practice, Teach, and All Tools without reading technical documentation.

