# 6. MAKE THE EXPERIENCE UNDERSTANDABLE WITHOUT STRONG READING ABILITY

This requirement is extremely important.

The design must work for users who primarily understand interfaces through pictures, symbols, familiar interaction patterns, and visual feedback.

Do not interpret this as permission to make an icon-only interface.

Use a combination of:

- Recognizable icons.
- Clear illustrations.
- Short, simple labels.
- Large, obvious action buttons.
- Visual examples.
- Consistent colors and visual meanings.
- Simple step-by-step guidance.
- Optional audio instructions.

### Audio guidance

Investigate whether browser-native speech synthesis can provide a useful optional way to read instructions, button descriptions, and short guidance aloud.

If supported, implement it without adding a paid API or external subscription.

Requirements:

- Never autoplay speech.
- Provide an obvious speaker/listen control.
- Allow users to stop speech.
- Keep written instructions available.
- Gracefully handle unsupported browsers.
- Do not make audio mandatory for completing any action.

### Visual interaction principles

Use clear action patterns such as:

- Explore
- Open
- Learn
- Watch
- Practice
- Save
- Continue
- Go Back

Do not require a user to understand complicated terminology before interacting.

For example:

Instead of displaying only "Physiology Simulation", explain:

"See how the heart and lungs work."

Instead of displaying only "Relationship Explorer", explain:

"See how body parts connect to one another."

Keep proper scientific terminology available within the content for advanced users.

The objective is to explain complex science simply without making the science inaccurate.

### Accessibility

Implement and verify:

- Readable text sizes.
- Strong color contrast.
- Clear active and selected states.
- Consistent icon meanings.
- Large touch targets.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Visible focus indicators.
- Accessible names and labels.
- Useful screen-reader semantics.
- No information communicated through color alone.
- Responsive layouts.

Never make a critical action dependent on hover.

